\chapter{Pixels}\label{ch:pixels}

Every numeric part of LineScope works on one image representation, the [[PixelBuffer]]. Choosing
it well settles a surprising number of later questions, so this chapter is short on code but long on
decisions.

\section{The representation}

A buffer stores its pixels \emph{interleaved}: the four components of a pixel sit next to each
other, and pixels follow one another row by row, starting with the \emph{top} row. The component
of channel $c \in \{0,1,2,3\}$ (red, green, blue, alpha) at column $x$ and row $y$ of a buffer
of width $W$ therefore lives at index
\[
  4\,(yW + x) + c .
\]
Row 0 at the top matches the way images are stored in files and drawn on the screen, so no
flipping is ever needed between the buffer, Core Graphics bitmaps and the views.

Components are 32-bit floats in $[0,1]$. Floats let a filter produce intermediate values
(negative lobes of a Lanczos kernel, overshoot of a sharpening filter) without wrapping around,
and they make the arithmetic in resampling exact enough that the tests can compare against
closed-form answers.

Two further conventions matter.

\paragraph{Encoded, not linear.} The components are the sRGB-\emph{encoded} values, the numbers
stored in the file and sent to the display, not the linear-light intensities obtained by undoing
the sRGB transfer curve. A physically careful resampler would linearize first; LineScope
deliberately does not, because its purpose is to show the profile of the values a user sees and
that most software filters operate on. Everything downstream (profiles, spectra, filters) sees
the same encoded numbers.

\paragraph{Straight alpha.} Colors are stored with \emph{straight} (non-premultiplied) alpha, so
a half-transparent red pixel is $(1, 0, 0, \tfrac12)$ rather than $(\tfrac12, 0, 0, \tfrac12)$.
Straight alpha is what a user expects to read in a profile. Filtering, however, must be done on
premultiplied values $(\alpha r, \alpha g, \alpha b, \alpha)$; otherwise a fully transparent
pixel, whose color is arbitrary, would bleed its color into its neighbors. The buffer therefore
offers conversions in both directions (Section~\ref{sec:premultiply}).

The file has two parts: the types themselves, and the bridge to Core Graphics.

