A single color sample is a small value type. It is what a line sampler returns and what the color
conversions of Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis} consume. Alpha defaults to opaque, because most samples are.

<<PixelBuffer: the color sample [[RGBA]]>>=
/// A color sample with straight (non-premultiplied) alpha, sRGB-encoded components in 0...1.
public struct RGBA: Sendable, Equatable {
    public var r: Float
    public var g: Float
    public var b: Float
    public var a: Float

    public init(r: Float, g: Float, b: Float, a: Float = 1) {
        self.r = r
        self.g = g
        self.b = b
        self.a = a
    }
}
@

The buffer is a struct holding its dimensions and one flat array. Swift arrays are copy-on-write,
so passing a buffer around (to a background task, into an [[ImageVariant]]) costs nothing until
someone mutates it. The type is [[Sendable]], which lets it cross from the main actor to a detached
task and back, and [[Equatable]], which the tests use to compare whole images.

<<PixelBuffer: the buffer type>>=
/// Interleaved RGBA Float32 image. Row 0 is the top row.
/// Components are sRGB-encoded (not linearized) with straight alpha, so values match what is displayed.
public struct PixelBuffer: Sendable, Equatable {
    <<PixelBuffer: stored properties and initializers>>

    <<PixelBuffer: pixel subscript>>

