\section{Construction and access}

The designated initializer takes the raw array and checks the one invariant everything else relies
on: exactly four floats per pixel, and at least one pixel. A buffer with zero width would make the
resampler divide by zero, so it is rejected at the door. The second initializer fills the image with
one color; the tests and the test-pattern generator start from it.

<<PixelBuffer: stored properties and initializers>>=
public let width: Int
public let height: Int
public var data: [Float]

public init(width: Int, height: Int, data: [Float]) {
    precondition(width > 0 && height > 0 && data.count == width * height * 4)
    self.width = width
    self.height = height
    self.data = data
}

public init(width: Int, height: Int, fill: RGBA = RGBA(r: 0, g: 0, b: 0, a: 1)) {
    var data = [Float](repeating: 0, count: width * height * 4)
    for i in stride(from: 0, to: data.count, by: 4) {
        data[i] = fill.r
        data[i + 1] = fill.g
        data[i + 2] = fill.b
        data[i + 3] = fill.a
    }
    self.init(width: width, height: height, data: data)
}
@

A subscript reads and writes whole pixels using the index formula above. It is convenient rather
than fast: the heavy loops in resampling and filtering index [[data]] directly through unsafe buffer
pointers instead.

<<PixelBuffer: pixel subscript>>=
public subscript(x: Int, y: Int) -> RGBA {
    get {
        let i = (y * width + x) * 4
        return RGBA(r: data[i], g: data[i + 1], b: data[i + 2], a: data[i + 3])
    }
    set {
        let i = (y * width + x) * 4
        data[i] = newValue.r
        data[i + 1] = newValue.g
        data[i + 2] = newValue.b
        data[i + 3] = newValue.a
    }
}
@

\section{Premultiplication}\label{sec:premultiply}

To premultiply, scale the three color components of every pixel by its alpha. The result is a bare
array, not a [[PixelBuffer]], on purpose: premultiplied data is an internal intermediate of the
resampler and of the Core Image bridge, and giving it its own type would invite someone to plot it.

Going back divides by alpha. Two safeguards make this robust against what filters produce. Alpha is
first clamped to $[0,1]$, since a Lanczos kernel can overshoot it. Where alpha is (nearly) zero the
color is undefined, and dividing would amplify rounding noise into bright garbage; such pixels
become transparent black. The color components are clamped too, which is where the overshoot and
undershoot of sharpening kernels is finally cut off.

<<PixelBuffer: premultiplication>>=
// MARK: Premultiplication (used by resampling and Core Image, which expect premultiplied data)

func premultiplied() -> [Float] {
    var out = data
    for i in stride(from: 0, to: out.count, by: 4) {
        let a = out[i + 3]
        out[i] *= a
        out[i + 1] *= a
        out[i + 2] *= a
    }
    return out
}

static func fromPremultiplied(width: Int, height: Int, _ p: [Float]) -> PixelBuffer {
    var out = p
    for i in stride(from: 0, to: out.count, by: 4) {
        let a = min(max(out[i + 3], 0), 1)
        out[i + 3] = a
        if a > 1e-6 {
            out[i] = min(max(out[i] / a, 0), 1)
            out[i + 1] = min(max(out[i + 1] / a, 0), 1)
            out[i + 2] = min(max(out[i + 2] / a, 0), 1)
        } else {
            out[i] = 0
            out[i + 1] = 0
            out[i + 2] = 0
        }
    }
    return PixelBuffer(width: width, height: height, data: out)
}
@

\section{Bridging to Core Graphics}

Images enter the program as [[CGImage]] values decoded by ImageIO, and they leave it as
[[CGImage]] values drawn by SwiftUI or written to PNG. Both directions go through the sRGB color
space, created once.

<<PixelBuffer: the sRGB color space>>=
static let sRGB = CGColorSpace(name: CGColorSpace.sRGB)!
@

A decoded image can have any layout: 16 bits per component, CMYK, a grayscale palette, alpha first
or last. Rather than handling each case, the decoder lets Core Graphics do the conversion: it
creates an 8-bit premultiplied RGBA bitmap in sRGB and draws the image into it. Interpolation is
switched off; the image is drawn at its own size, so no resampling should happen at all, and
this makes sure of it. The bitmap's memory is borrowed from a Swift array for the duration of the
closure, and the bytes are then scaled to $[0,1]$ and un-premultiplied.

This does lose precision for 16-bit sources. The inspector reports the source's real bit depth, so
the user knows when that happened.

<<PixelBuffer: decoding a [[CGImage]]>>=
/// Decodes any CGImage into 8-bit sRGB and converts it to a float buffer.
public init?(cgImage: CGImage) {
    let w = cgImage.width
    let h = cgImage.height
    guard w > 0, h > 0 else { return nil }
    var bytes = [UInt8](repeating: 0, count: w * h * 4)
    let drawn = bytes.withUnsafeMutableBytes { raw -> Bool in
        guard let ctx = CGContext(
            data: raw.baseAddress, width: w, height: h, bitsPerComponent: 8, bytesPerRow: w * 4,
            space: PixelBuffer.sRGB, bitmapInfo: CGImageAlphaInfo.premultipliedLast.rawValue
        ) else { return false }
        ctx.interpolationQuality = .none
        ctx.draw(cgImage, in: CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: w, height: h))
        return true
    }
    guard drawn else { return nil }
    var p = [Float](repeating: 0, count: w * h * 4)
    for i in 0..<p.count { p[i] = Float(bytes[i]) / 255 }
    self = PixelBuffer.fromPremultiplied(width: w, height: h, p)
}
@

The opposite direction clamps, premultiplies and rounds each component to a byte, and wraps the
bytes in a data provider. The image is marked [[shouldInterpolate: false]] so that when SwiftUI
draws it, nothing smooths the pixels behind the program's back. Seeing the blocky result of
nearest-neighbor upsampling is part of the point.

<<PixelBuffer: encoding a [[CGImage]]>>=
/// Renders the buffer as an 8-bit premultiplied sRGB CGImage for display or export.
public func makeCGImage() -> CGImage? {
    var bytes = [UInt8](repeating: 0, count: data.count)
    for i in stride(from: 0, to: data.count, by: 4) {
        let a = min(max(data[i + 3], 0), 1)
        bytes[i] = UInt8((min(max(data[i], 0), 1) * a * 255).rounded())
        bytes[i + 1] = UInt8((min(max(data[i + 1], 0), 1) * a * 255).rounded())
        bytes[i + 2] = UInt8((min(max(data[i + 2], 0), 1) * a * 255).rounded())
        bytes[i + 3] = UInt8((a * 255).rounded())
    }
    guard let provider = CGDataProvider(data: Data(bytes) as CFData) else { return nil }
    return CGImage(
        width: width, height: height, bitsPerComponent: 8, bitsPerPixel: 32, bytesPerRow: width * 4,
        space: PixelBuffer.sRGB, bitmapInfo: CGBitmapInfo(rawValue: CGImageAlphaInfo.premultipliedLast.rawValue),
        provider: provider, decode: nil, shouldInterpolate: false, intent: .defaultIntent
    )
}
@
